capability request/multipart-read  a multipart body read by a server
  heroes  http.parts_of, http.parse_multipart, http.part_of, http.FormPart, http.PartLimits, http.default_part_limits
  go      net/http.Request.ParseMultipartForm, net/http.Request.MultipartReader, net/http.Request.FormFile, net/http.Request.MultipartForm, mime/multipart.NewReader, mime/multipart.Reader.NextPart, NextRawPart, ReadForm, mime/multipart.Form, FileHeader, Part.FormName, Part.FileName, mime/multipart.ErrMessageTooLarge
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    every part held in memory within `PartLimits` (1,000 parts as Go's `multipartmaxparts` and werkzeug's `max_form_parts`; heads of 16 KiB; 10 MiB a part and a body), `limit` past one, where Go spills files to the disk past `maxMemory`; observed against Go 1.27's `mime/multipart` and werkzeug 3.1.9's `parse_form_data` on 2026-11-17: line feeds alone, a preamble, an epilogue, spaces after a boundary, folded head lines, `filename*` over `filename` and an empty `filename` read as both read them; `%22`, `%0D` and `%0A` in a name read back as werkzeug reads them (Go keeps them); a file name kept as written, `a/b/../c.txt` whole, as werkzeug keeps it, where Go's `FileName` keeps its last part by the platform's own separator; a body with no closing boundary, a part with no `form-data` disposition or no name, and a name given twice refused `invalid`, as werkzeug refuses them, where Go reads on; Python's standard library lost `cgi.FieldStorage` in 3.13 (`import cgi` fails on 3.15), and `email`'s parser is `mail`'s yardstick

