  note    added 2026-11-17. An `http://` request is written to the proxy with its whole URL as its target (RFC 9112 § 3.2.2), an `https://` one through a tunnel opened at `CONNECT` (RFC 9110 § 9.3.6) with TLS over it by `tls.client_over`, the server's own name checked and no byte of the request shown to the proxy; a refused tunnel fails `failed` with the proxy's status, as requests' ProxyError. `tls.client_over` is `unsupported` on macOS, whose TLS runs only over a connection Network.framework makes, so on macOS an `https://` request through a proxy fails `unsupported` and says why, where `tls.connect_after`'s exchange before TLS cannot serve, since it checks the certificate against the host it connects to, the proxy. A proxy's user and password are sent as `Proxy-Authorization: Basic`; an `https://` or SOCKS proxy is `unsupported`. The environment is read only by `proxy_from_env`, the lower case variable first as curl and Python read it (Go reads the capitals first), `HTTP_PROXY` not read under CGI (httpoxy) as Go and Python refuse it; `NO_PROXY` as Go reads it, names, `.`-names, addresses, networks and ports, `localhost` and loopback always direct as Go sends them. Proxied requests run on this Mac against a proxy of the tests' own (the absolute-form target, the credentials, a 407, a tunnel then TLS refused `unsupported`); through a real proxy, and an `https://` one on Linux and Windows: unrun. macOS's system proxy settings (`getproxies_macosx_sysconf`, `proxy_bypass_macosx_sysconf`) are not read: a program reads them as it reads the environment, by its own choice

